### The Issue

A minimum example that was added to the e2e tests:
```sway
contract;

pub struct SomeStruct {
    par1: u32,
}

pub struct AllStruct {
    some_struct: SomeStruct,
}

abi MyContract {
    fn get_struct() -> AllStruct;
    fn check_struct_integrity(arg: AllStruct) -> bool;
}

impl MyContract for Contract {
    fn get_struct() -> AllStruct {
        AllStruct {
            some_struct: SomeStruct {
                par1: 12345u32,
            },
        }
    }
    fn check_struct_integrity(arg: AllStruct) -> bool {
        arg.some_struct.par1 == 12345u32
    }
}
```

If you proceeded to call `get_struct` you'd get:
```
'Failed to run `new_from_tokens()` for custom SomeStruct struct (tokens have wrong order and/or wrong types): InvalidOutputType("Expected `u32`, got Struct([U32(12345)])")'
```

The original error from the issue can be reproduced as well but I decided to use u32 to make the minimal example simpler.

The token representation of `AllStruct` is as follows:
```
[Struct([Struct([U32(12345)])])]
```
When we try to instantiate AllStruct the process goes through
```rust
impl<T: Tokenizable> Detokenize for T {
    fn from_tokens(mut tokens: Vec<Token>) -> Result<Self, InvalidOutputType> {
        let token = match tokens.len() {
            0 => Token::Struct(vec![]),
            1 => tokens.remove(0),
            _ => Token::Struct(tokens),
        };

        Self::from_token(token)
    }
}
```

Which strips away the first `Struct` and forwards 
```
[Struct([U32(12345)])]
```

 to `::from_token` in `AllStruct`.
 
The problem is that the `::from_token` implementations are not suited to do any further stripping away of `Struct` wrappers and they just delegate what they receive to their subcomponents. Here the `Struct` token in whole is given to `U32::from_token` which causes an issue.

### About the solution

We have multiple traits and methods that are connected with tokenization. 

Tokenizable - which can tokenize and detokenize
Detokenize - which can just detokenize
Parameterize - which can also detokenize, and does some other stuff as well

I've removed `Detokenize` and the detokenizing logic from `Parameterize`.

The only thing needed for a type to be able to be tokenized or detokenized is to implement `Tokenizable`.

I've adapted the code generation to make the Tokenizable implementation self-sufficient without it delegating to `Parameterized`. 

I've also changed the interface to accept *a* Token rather than a slice of Tokens, as one type can *only* ever be constructed from one token. Having a slice that contained the right token at the start, and a bunch of other ones you don't need only confused the issue.

I've also laid most of the groundwork for #358 , since the 'ability' to have multiple function outputs was probably the reason why the interface accepted a slice of Tokens.

Although there was always a check that we either have 0 or 1 return arguments, it is now explicit through the usage of 'Option' rather than a `Vec`.

Almost all `.clone()`s have been eliminated thanks to the change in the interface -- the detokenization process now consumes the tokens given to it.
